  completeExplanation: 'Chapter 7 of Ganita Prakash redefines how students perceive fractions, shifting away from superficial "shaded parts" diagrams toward rigorous measurement via fractional units (unit fractions 1/n).\n\nThe chapter begins with everyday sharing of rotis, sugarcane juice, and chikki. A unit fraction 1/n represents one equal share when a whole unit is divided into n parts. The text directly tackles the widespread student misconception that "9 > 5 so 1/9 > 1/5", showing through physical sharing that dividing among more recipients inevitably yields smaller individual portions (1/5 > 1/9, 1/100 > 1/200). Fractions are then shown to be counts of unit pieces: 3/4 is simply 3 times the unit 1/4. The denominator names the fractional unit; the numerator counts how many such units are taken.\n\nSection 7.4-7.5 maps fractions onto the continuous number line by subdividing the interval between whole integers. Fractions less than 1 (proper) have numerator < denominator, while fractions greater than 1 (improper) have numerator > denominator and are decomposed into mixed fractions (e.g. 8/3 = 2 + 2/3 = 2 2/3; 47/9 = 5 2/9).\n\nSection 7.6 uses the visual "Fraction Wall" to illustrate Equivalent Fractions-different expressions for the same physical length (1/2 = 2/4 = 3/6 = 4/8 = 5/10). Multiplicative equivalence ((a x k)/(b x k) = a/b) is established, leading directly to Simplest Form / Lowest Terms by dividing top and bottom by their HCF.\n\nSections 7.7 and 7.8 explore Comparing Fractions and Brahmagupta\'s Method for Addition and Subtraction (628 CE). Unlike fractions are first transformed into equivalent fractions with a common denominator (using LCM), after which numerators are added or subtracted directly before final reduction.\n\nFinally, Section 7.9 provides rich historical and cultural depth: Sanskrit origins (bhinna = broken, amsha/bhaga = part), the ancient Bakshali manuscript (c. 300 CE), Indian masters (Aryabhata, Brahmagupta, Mahaviracharya), Moroccan mathematician Al-Hassar\'s invention of the horizontal fraction bar, and Egyptian unit fraction puzzles (1/2 + 1/3 + 1/6 = 1).',
